2d/3d CT image processing and filtering\
\
***NOTE: THE PROJECT IS NOT TESTED ON WINDOWS AND IS NOT CONFIRMED TO WORK AND HENCE I ADVISE TO USE EITHER MAC OR LINUX TO TEST THE PROJECT, THE REASON FOR SUCH AN ISSUE IS THAT I MYSELF USED A MAC TO MAKE THIS PROJECT\
\
\
Requirements:\
c++ 17 compiler(Clang, GCC)\
CMake 3.x or newer\
Internet acess on the first configure(only if libtiff is not installed)\
Optional: python 3.9+, pybind11 and numpy\
\
Building the project:\
\
From the project root folder(type cd /project file path for mac in terminal)\
Now type the following 2 commands:\
\
cmake -B build -S . -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release\
cmake  --build build -j\
\
the above commands have to be run only once.\
\
now you can start using the project, \
\
interactive mode:\
\
type the following command in the terminal from the project root folder\
\
./build/ctfilter\
\
now you can enter the path of the file you want to filter\
after this you can select any of the filter or all of the filters, when you select individually you will also have the option of giving your parameters for the respective filters, else there are default parameters built in\
\
Using directly with arguments:\cf3 \
\
\cf2 ./build/ctfilter --in /path/to/slice.tif --filter gaussian \cf3 \
\cf2 ./build/ctfilter --in /path/to/slice.tif --filter all \cf3 \
\cf2 ./build/ctfilter --in-dir /path/to/slices/ --filter gaussian --mode 3d \cf3 \
\cf2 \
--in takes a single TIFF image, --in-dir takes a folder of slices. --mode 3d filters the whole volume; the default is slice-by-slice. Results are written to ./ct_output/ (or --output-root <folder>).}